n\n2, 9, 3, 3, 3, 5, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 9, 3, 5, 7, 3, 5, 3, 3, 3, 5, 3, 3, 7, 7, 3, 7, 5, 3, 5, 5, 9, 5, 3, 3, 5, 3, 3, 11, 9, 5, 21, 5, 9, 3, 9, 3, 5, 55, 3, 7, 27, 9, 27, 7, 5, 5, 3, 9, 3, 3, 3, 5, 3, 7, 7, 11, 3, 3, 3, 5, 5, 7, 7, 3, 5, 3, 9, 3, 3, 5, 11, 3, 5, 47, 5, 3, 3, 5, 3, 3, 5, 7, 3, 3, 7, 3, 5, 5, 5, 3\n\n(list; graph; refs; listen; history; text; internal format)\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,1\n\nLINKS\n\nCharles R Greathouse IV, Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000\n\nEdwige Tolla, Conjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes, arXiv:2601.15346 [math.GM], 2026.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nFor n=1, start-prime = prime(1) = 2, 2+3=5 is prime, length=2, so a(1)=2;\n\nfor n=2, start-prime = prime(2) = 3, 3 + 5 + 7 + 11 + 13 + 17 + 19 + 23 + 29 = 127 is prime, length=9, all shorter partial sums are composite, so a(2)=9;\n\nfor n=160, prime(160) = 941, 941 + ... + 1609 = 121123 is prime, a(160)=95.\n\nMATHEMATICA\n\nTable[k = 2; While[CompositeQ@ Total@ Prime@ Range[n, n + k], k++]; k + 2 Boole[EvenQ@ k] - 1, {n, 120}] (* Michael De Vlieger, Jan 01 2017 *)\n\nPROG\n\n(PARI) a(n, p=prime(n))=my(q=p, t=2); while(!isprime(p+=q=nextprime(q+1)), t++); t\n\napply(p->a(0, p), primes(30)) \\\\ Charles R Greathouse IV, Jun 16 2015\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A071195, A071196, A071197, A071198.\n\nSequence in context: A375771 A011066 A388484 * A347359 A346839 A137616\n\nAdjacent sequences: A071191 A071192 A071193 * A071195 A071196 A071197\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn\n\nAUTHOR\n\nLabos Elemer, May 16 2002\n\nEXTENSIONS\n\nEscape clause added by N. J. A. Sloane, Nov 17 2020\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/de/abs/2601.15346)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; @misc{tolla2026conjecturessumsconsecutiveprimes, title={Conjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes}, author={Edwige Tolla}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.15346}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15346}, }\nSubmitted 20 Jan 2026\n\n# Conjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes\n\nET\n\nEdwige Tolla\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study additive properties of consecutive prime numbers and the primality of the sums they generate. For a given prime number $p_n$p n​, we consider the sums $$ S_k(p_n) = p_n + p_{n+1} + \\cdots + p_{n+k-1}, $$S k​(p n​)=p n​+p n+1​+⋯+p n+k−1​, where $k \\ge 3$k≥3 is an odd integer. We first formulate an existence conjecture asserting that, for every prime number $p_n$p n​, there exists at least one odd length $k \\ge 3$k≥3 such that $S_k(p_n)$S k​(p n​) is itself a prime number. An exhaustive computational verification covering the first one million prime numbers revealed no counterexamples. We then propose a strengthened conjecture according to which, for every prime number $p_n$p n​, there exist infinitely many odd lengths $k$k such that $S_k(p_n)$S k​(p n​) is prime. This strong version is supported by a probabilistic heuristic showing that the series of the corresponding primality probabilities diverges, suggesting that the phenomenon is not exceptional but recurrent. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral Gaps on Large Hyperbolic Surfaces | ArxivLens (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/spectral-gaps-on-large-hyperbolic-surfaces-2800-ca5fcef0)\nciteturn5search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; 2601.13988 Published Jan 20, 2026\n\n# Spectral Gaps on Large Hyperbolic Surfaces\n\nFrédéric Naud, Laura Monk\n\n2601.13988 Published Jan 20, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this expository paper, we review the history and the recent breakthroughs in the spectral theory of large volume hyperbolic surfaces. More precisely, we focus mostly on the investigation of the first non-trivial eigenvalue $λ_1$ and its possible behaviour in the large volume regime.\n\nResults What the authors found\n\n  * Establishment of a spectral gap of 3/16 - ε for random hyperbolic surfaces\n  * Development of Friedman-Ramanujan functions to model eigenvalue distributions\n  * Proof of near-optimal spectral gaps for large genus surfaces\n\n\nLimitations Where the findings need caution\n\n  * The analysis focuses primarily on surfaces with large genus and specific geometric conditions\n  * The results rely on complex technical assumptions about random models and their convergence properties\n\nMethodology How the work was done\n\nThe research employs a combination of probabilistic methods, spectral geometry, and random matrix theory to analyze the spectral properties of random hyperbolic surfaces. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Tolla_E in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ATolla_E+in%3Amath)\nciteturn5search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Jan 23 2026 math.GM arXiv:2601.15346v1\n\n1 results for au:Tolla_E in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * Conjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes\n\nEdwige Tolla\n\nJan 23 2026 math.GM arXiv:2601.15346v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2601.15346, author = {Edwige Tolla}, title = {{C}onjectures on {S}ums of {C}onsecutive {P}rimes}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2601.15346}, note = {arXiv:2601.15346v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study additive properties of consecutive prime numbers and the primality of the sums they generate. For a given prime number $p_n$, we consider the sums \\[ S_k(p_n) = p_n + p_n+1 + ⋯+ p_n+k-1, \\]where $k \\ge 3$ is an odd integer. We first formulate an existence conjecture asserting that, for every prime number $p_n$, there exists at least one odd length $k \\ge 3$ such that $S_k(p_n)$ is itself a prime number. An exhaustive computational verification covering the first one million prime numbers revealed no counterexamples. We then propose a strengthened conjecture according to which, for every prime number $p_n$, there exist infinitely many odd lengths $k$ such that $S_k(p_n)$ is prime. This strong version is supported by a probabilistic heuristic showing that the series of the corresponding primality probabilities diverges, suggesting that the phenomenon is not exceptional but recurrent. We also analyze the possible modular obstructions, showing that they are local in nature and cannot persist when the length $k$ varies among odd integers. A Diophantine interpretation of the problem is proposed, together with a conceptual comparison with the generalized Goldbach conjecture. Finally, we discuss the role of the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH) in controlling the distribution of the sums under consideration. These structural, modular, Diophantine, and probabilistic (heuristic) arguments support both conjectures and formalize heuristic theorems of Cramér, GRH, and Hardy--Littlewood type explaining the expected absence of counterexamples.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2601.15346)\nciteturn5search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; # 2601.15346\n# 2601.15346\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 Conjectures on Sums of Consecutive Primes [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthor: Edwige Tolla\n\nWe study additive properties of consecutive prime numbers and the primality of the sums they generate. For a given prime number $p_n$, we consider the sums \\[ S_k(p_n) = p_n + p_{n+1} + \\cdots + p_{n+k-1}, \\] where $k \\ge 3$ is an odd integer. We first formulate an existence conjecture asserting that, for every prime number $p_n$, there exists at least one odd length $k \\ge 3$ such that $S_k(p_n)$ is itself a prime number. An exhaustive computational verification covering the first one million prime numbers revealed no counterexamples. We then propose a strengthened conjecture according to which, for every prime number $p_n$, there exist infinitely many odd lengths $k$ such that $S_k(p_n)$ is prime. This strong version is supported by a probabilistic heuristic showing that the series of the corresponding primality probabilities diverges, suggesting that the phenomenon is not exceptional but recurrent. We also analyze the possible modular obstructions, showing that they are local in nature and cannot persist when the length $k$ varies among odd integers. A Diophantine interpretation of the problem is proposed, together with a conceptual comparison with the generalized Goldbach conjecture. Finally, we discuss the role of the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH) in controlling the distribution of the sums under consideration. These structural, modular, Diophantine, and probabilistic (heuristic) arguments support both conjectures and formalize heuristic theorems of Cramér, GRH, and Hardy--Littlewood type explaining the expected absence of counterexamples.\n\nSubject: General Mathematics\n\nPublish: 2026-01-20 20:14:34 UTC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterizations of monadically dependent tree-ordered weakly sparse structures | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.16039v1)\nciteturn5search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{buffière2026characterizationsmonadicallydependenttreeordered, title={Characterizations of monadically dependent tree-ordered weakly sparse structures}, author={Hector Buffière and Yuquan Lin and Jaroslav Nešetřil and Patrice Ossona de Mendez and Sebastian Siebertz}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.16039}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DM}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.16039}, }\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArxiver (https://arxiver.lazybrains.com/author/84082)\nciteturn5search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; arXiv:2601.13988\n--------------------------------------------------